Z||000007||jerpro||11-24-2000||02:54 PM||||Check engile light went finally on... It is giving me 4 codes.<BR>33,34,44 and 45.<BR>What is the MAP sensor? where is it located at? how do I go by fixing it?<BR>Is it possible to have error codes 44 and 45 altoghether?!?!? ONe is lean exhaust and the other one is rich exhaust. <BR>Please help.<BR>||||reg||9
Z||000008||StuGood||11-24-2000||03:44 PM||||MAP sensor is manifold absolute pressure sensor (essentially, reports manifold vacuum to the ECM).  It's a more-or-less flat, rectangular plastic thing with a vacuum line to it, and an electrical connector.<P>On 4-cyl Fieros, it's on the side of air cleaner.  On 6-cyl Fieros, it's on the RHS of the plenum.  It's easy to get to and change.<P>That much, I'm pretty sure of.  Since you're getting codes 44 <B>and</B> 45 (lean and rich), I <B>guess</B> the oxygen sensor might be suspect(?) - I believe those have been known to go bad, but again, I haven't any direct experience with that.  Oxygen sensor isn't as easy to swap out as the MAP, so 'fit were me, I'd try the MAP first.<P>Warning: I'm giving you a lot of guesswork here, at this point!  Hopefully, when you find the MAP, you'll find something cheap and obvious - like, the vacuum line fell off the MAP  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.  <BR><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 11-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||PlasticWrapd||11-27-2000||10:14 PM||||Fuel filter.  What's happening is the car leans out when you punch it, because of the lack of fuel.  The computer tries to compensate by firing the injector more frequently.  When you let off, the injector is still firing at the increased frequency, but the air flow is reduced, creating a rich condition.  It takes a few seconds to clear the extra fuel and in this time, the computer reads an overly rich condition via the o2 sensor.  This the code 44 and 45 at the same time.  The map sensor code is set because you are lowering the manifold vacuum (pressing the throttle), but the engine RPM is not increasing fast enough for the corrresponding sensor reading.  The ECM reads a low signal from the map sensor that doesn't correspond with the appropriate increase in engine RPM.  So the ECM "thinks" that the map sensor has failed.  Rule of thumb, replace the cheapest part first!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
